## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a cross-tenant tool governance layer for an LLM-ops gateway with external integrations. It probes policy modeling, isolation, and auditability under realistic latency constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant policy enforcement
- Tool-usage planning and data-scope
- Sandboxed tool execution and per-tenant credentials
- Tamper-evident, signed audit logs
- Latency targets and test plans

## Code Example

```javascript
function evaluatePolicy(prompt, tenantId, toolList) {
  const policy = fetchPolicyForTenant(tenantId);
  // allowlist check
  const allowed = toolList.filter(t => policy.allowedTools.includes(t));
  // data scope check
  const dataOk = policy.dataScope.includes(extractDataScope(prompt));
  // rate limiting
  enforceRateLimit(tenantId, prompt);
  return { allowed, dataOk };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle tool failures or partial failures within a policy?
- How to evolve policies safely across tenants without breaking existing prompts?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about lightweight, policy-driven routing in a multi-tenant LLM ops context. Focuses on practical data modeling, decision logic, and test coverage for beginners.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant routing state (budget, privacy)
- Latency estimation and budget checks
- Policy-compliant model selection and auditing
- Basic test plan with reproducible scenarios

## Code Example
```javascript
function routeForTenant(tenant, models, estimates, budgets){
  // pick fastest model within budget and privacy constraints
  const allowed = models.filter(m => tenant.allowedModels.includes(m.name) && m.privacyOK === true);
  const feasible = allowed.filter(m => estimates[m.name] <= tenant.latencyBudgetMs);
  return feasible.sort((a,b) => estimates[a.name]-estimates[b.name])[0] || null;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you simulate burst traffic and verify SLA adherence?
- How would you adapt routing when a model becomes unavailable or budget changes mid-session?
